WEBVTT

00:00.250 --> 00:03.946
Water is a map problem before it is a numbers
problem —

00:04.066 --> 00:09.226
what your neighbour's well is doing matters to
yours, and so does where the rain fell.

00:09.346 --> 00:13.066
MyWell has two maps, and they work the same
way.

00:13.883 --> 00:16.811
Now we're on: Stations on map.

00:16.931 --> 00:19.595
Every shared station in one place.

00:19.715 --> 00:26.411
Filter, search, group stations into Monitoring Areas, and
read a chart of whatever the map is showing.

00:27.217 --> 00:29.329
Open a monitoring area.

00:29.449 --> 00:33.457
A monitoring area is a saved set of stations
you follow together.

00:33.577 --> 00:39.841
We've opened the sample ‘Badgaon village’ area for
you — see the map focus on just its stations.

00:39.961 --> 00:43.321
Pick any area here to scope the whole page to
it.

00:44.150 --> 00:45.830
Filter by type.

00:45.950 --> 00:53.054
Open Station type to show or hide each kind of
station — groundwater wells, rainfall, check-dams,

00:53.174 --> 00:54.854
rivers and more.

00:54.974 --> 00:57.326
It doubles as the map legend.

00:58.150 --> 01:00.430
Find specific stations.

01:00.550 --> 01:06.646
Use Station to search by name or code and pick just
the stations you want; the map narrows to them.

01:07.450 --> 01:09.082
Build an area.

01:09.202 --> 01:15.250
Actions is how you create an area: Pick
stations one by one, Draw an area on the map,

01:15.370 --> 01:18.250
or Select area by dragging a box.

01:18.370 --> 01:21.658
Save it and it appears in My Monitoring Areas.

01:22.483 --> 01:24.307
Now see its data.

01:24.427 --> 01:27.835
Once an area is picked, this card appears.

01:27.955 --> 01:34.147
Open Data trend for its readings charted over time,
or Data statistics for a ranked breakdown.

01:34.267 --> 01:37.531
Your own saved areas open the same way.

01:38.350 --> 01:40.294
That is the whole page.

01:40.414 --> 01:45.070
You can replay this tutorial any time from the
Learning page inside the app.

01:45.190 --> 01:47.854
Thanks for helping look after our water.
